WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.870
Sinister is one of the better big-budget horror
movies in recent memory, and nobody was surprised

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.360
when this Blumhouse film got the sequel treatment
in 2015.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.880
But since then, there hasn't been so much
as a whisper about a third chapter.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.820
Instead, it looks like the whole Sinister
series has been abandoned in favor of other

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:16.820
franchises.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.930
Why does sequel-happy Hollywood want nothing
more to do with Bughuul and his box of haunted

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.930
videos?

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:22.930
Let's roll the tape.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.369
Sinister 2 didn't make enough cash

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:28.880
Producer Jason Blum knows a thing or two about
getting a series off the ground.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.090
He's the man who made one-off movies like
Paranormal Activity, Insidious, and The Purge

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.320
into successful, multi-film franchises.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.579
But when asked about the prospect of a third
Sinister movie during a Facebook Live chat

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:44.660
in 2016, Blum was brutally honest, saying,
"We didn't do well enough with Sinister 2.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.620
Not enough people went to see Sinister 2 to
make Sinister 3, which is really sad."

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.440
If you're wondering what "sad" looks like
in terms of cold, hard numbers: the original

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.629
Sinister made $77 million on a $3 million
budget; the sequel, which cost more than three

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:01.590
times as much to make, brought in only $52
million.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.399
Critics tore the second film apart

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:07.170
So why did audiences not latch onto Sinister
2 the way that they did for the original?

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.120
Not to be blunt, but probably because it was
a bad movie — at least according to critics.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.820
Wesley Morris of Grantland summed it up best,
writing, "There comes a point when you have

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.570
to say no to these horror sequels.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.650
There's no skill or idea visible anywhere,
just the sound of money being sucked from

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:22.650
your pockets."

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.600
And he wasn't alone; even critics who liked
the first Sinister movie thought this one

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.920
was cheap, unbelievable, and most of all,
unnecessary.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.880
The sequel botched the best part

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.290
Like the first movie, Sinister 2 centers around
a series of snuff films, each involving the

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.220
grisly murder of what appears to be an entire
family.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.000
And in the first film, these sequences were
so disturbing that it kinda felt like we shouldn't

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.840
even be seeing them — which made it a highly
effective horror movie, if not the source

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.260
of a few recurring nightmares.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.380
But in Sinister 2, the murder films looked
more like crudely shot B-movies, screened

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.610
with a bargain-bin horror soundtrack that
took them over the line from creepy to cheesy.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:05.600
With the scariest part of Sinister effectively
ruined by the sequel, it's no surprise that

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.360
Hollywood preferred not to unbox any future
home videos from Bughuul.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.310
They killed the premise — and the whole
cast

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.360
After the original Sinister ended with a huge
reveal — that the movie's snuff films were

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.180
made by kids, who killed their own families
while under the influence of an ancient Boogeyman

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.970
— Sinister 2 tried to do something different.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.940
With the source of the movies no longer a
mystery, the sequel took a new angle, focusing

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.920
on one kid as he's courted by the ghostly
squad of child murderers who want him to join

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.570
their ranks… and taking all the mystery
out of the premise that made Sinister scary

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.750
in the first place.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.750
Oops.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.439
On top of that, both of these movies end with
the deaths of virtually every major character

00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:44.200
— which is appropriately dark for a horror
film, but not so good for a horror franchise.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.510
With nobody left alive to star in a new installment,
Sinister 3 would have to start from scratch,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.400
creating a whole new set of characters to
breathe life into a tired storyline.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.660
The monster is a bore

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:58.080
This is a problem that started with Sinister
and never got fixed: Bughuul is more than

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.730
just an unpronounceable name —he's a lousy
character, too.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:09.470
"Some cultures believe that it lived in another
realm, reachable only by ritual or sacrifice."

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.130
Whatever that means.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:15.370
Both movies identify Bughuul, also known as
the Boogeyman, as an otherworldly being who

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.510
exists to corrupt innocent children.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.790
But as the antagonist of a major horror franchise,
we never really get to know Bughuul: his hopes,

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.990
his dreams, his reasons for dressing up like
a low-rent Michael Jackson impersonator.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.850
Sure, he makes for a good jump scare — but
without a compelling villain to shape the

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.100
franchise around, making more Sinister movies
would be a chore, especially when competing

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.130
series like The Conjuring are trotting out
fresh, interesting monsters with legitimate

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:40.780
backstories on the regular.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.650
The original director has moved on

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.510
Sinister director Scott Derrickson doesn't
do generic horror, and he's especially good

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.780
at translating classic subject matter into
something freshly terrifying.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.440
[Unintelligible]
"You speak Latin, huh?"

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.650
That would make him the perfect person to
get this spooky series back on track — if

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.280
only he had the time for it.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.090
But Derrickson let someone else take the reins
when it came to directing Sinister 2.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.850
And by the time the sequel hit theaters, he
was already on to bigger things: specifically

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:14.290
the Marvel movie Doctor Strange, which catapulted
him into a whole new world of big-budget,

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:15.580
high-profile projects.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.980
Between adapting Bong-Joon Ho's Snowpiercer
into a TV series and probably helming the

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.440
inevitable Doctor Strange sequel, Derrickson
has plenty on his plate — and he isn't likely

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.610
to waste his energy trying to bring small-budget
Bughuul and his squad of killer kiddos back

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.980
from the dead… or wherever they are.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.500
So when it comes to more Sinister sequels,
it looks like the hero from Sinister 2 gets

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.550
the last word.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.220
"It's over, Zach.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.020
You're not making another film."

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.020
Thanks for watching!

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.120
